# Bearer token check

The nx-fhir `auth-resource` feature adds a bearer token check to this server. The check applies to requests under `/fhir`. When `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, a request needs a JSON Web Token (JWT) from the issuer of `app.auth.issuer`. The request rule below lists the requests that pass with no token.

## What the feature adds

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/BearerTokenInterceptor.java` | The check. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceProperties.java` | The configuration properties under `app.auth`. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceConfig.java` | Registers the check on the FHIR server. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/IssuerMetadata.java` | Reads the documents under `{issuer}/.well-known/` and keeps each document after its first successful read. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/FhirBaseUrl.java` | Gives the base URL of the FHIR endpoint of this server. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/TrustAllSslContext.java` | The SSL context that accepts any certificate and any host name. The HTTP clients of the auth features use it when `app.auth.ssl-verify` is `false`. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/WellKnownInterceptor.java` | Answers a `GET` request for one document under `{baseURL}/.well-known/`. The auth features that publish such a document register it. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/BearerTokenInterceptorTest.java` | Unit test of the check with a local issuer. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/FhirBaseUrlTest.java` | Unit test of the base URL rule. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/TestCertificates.java` | Makes self-signed certificates for the unit tests with `keytool`. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceIT.java` | Integration test of the registration. It runs under `mvn verify`. |
| `src/main/resources/application.yaml` | The `app.auth` entries. |
| `src/test/resources/config/application.yaml` | `app.auth.enabled: false` for each Spring Boot test. |
| `src/test/resources/application-test.yaml` | `app.auth.enabled: false` for a test with the `test` profile. |

The two test configuration files turn the check off. A test that sets `app.auth.enabled` to `true` runs with the check.

## Configuration

```yaml
app:
  auth:
    enabled: true
    issuer: https://issuer.example.org
    audience: https://fhir.example.org/fhir
    jwks-uri: https://issuer.example.org/keys
    public-endpoints:
      - $bulk-publish
      - Practitioner
      - Patient/$match
    ssl-verify: true
    # bypass-header: X-Bypass-Auth
```

The install replaces `app.auth` with one map. The map has `enabled: true`, `issuer`, `audience` when the option has a value, `public-endpoints: []`, `ssl-verify`, and the comment line of `bypass-header`. The default issuer of the generator is `https://udap-security.fast.hl7.org`, the public FAST security server.

### `app.auth.enabled`

- `true`: the server checks each request under `/fhir`. `app.auth.issuer` must have a value. With no value, the application stops at startup.
- `false` or absent: the server has no check and ignores the other `app.auth` properties.

### `app.auth.issuer`

The `iss` claim of a token must be equal to `app.auth.issuer`.

### `app.auth.audience`

`audience` is optional. When it is set, the `aud` claim must contain it.

### `app.auth.jwks-uri`

`jwks-uri` is optional. When it is set, the server reads the signing keys from this URL and reads no discovery document. Use it for an issuer whose `iss` value is not a URL that the server can reach.

When `jwks-uri` is absent, the server reads `{issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration` and uses its `jwks_uri`. The `issuer` member of that document must be equal to `app.auth.issuer`. The server reads the document at the first token and keeps it after a successful read. After a failed read, the server reads the document again at the next token. The server starts and runs while the issuer is down.

The discovery read and the key set read each have a connect timeout of 5 seconds and a read timeout of 10 seconds.

### `app.auth.public-endpoints`

A request that fits an entry passes with no token. Each entry is relative to the FHIR base and has one of three forms:

- `Type`, for example `Practitioner`. Each request for that resource type passes. This includes a read, a search, a create, an update, a delete, and an operation on the type.
- `$operation`, for example `$bulk-publish`. A request for that server-level operation passes.
- `Type/$operation`, for example `Patient/$match`. A request for that operation on the type or on an instance of the type passes.

A public entry applies to each request method. The server compares an entry with the resource type and the operation that HAPI FHIR reads from the request. When `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, an entry in a different form stops the application at startup.

### `app.auth.ssl-verify`

- `true` or absent: the server checks the certificate chain and the host name for each call that the auth features make.
- `false`: the server does not check the certificate chain or the host name for these calls.
- When it is `false` and `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, the server writes one WARN line at startup.

The install writes `ssl-verify: false` when the issuer is an `https` URL with the host `localhost`. For each other issuer, the install writes `ssl-verify: true`.

WARNING: With `app.auth.ssl-verify: false`, the server accepts any certificate from the hosts it calls. Use it only with a local development certificate.

### `app.auth.bypass-header`

- `bypass-header` is optional. Its value is a header name.
- When it is set, a request that has this header skips the check. The header can have any value.
- When it is absent or empty, the server has no bypass.
- When it is set and `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, the server writes one WARN line at startup that names the header.

The install writes the comment line `# bypass-header: X-Bypass-Auth`, and the bypass is off. To turn on the bypass, remove `# ` from that line.

WARNING: With `app.auth.bypass-header` set, each caller that sends this header skips the token check. Remove the property for a deployment that has data that is not public.

## Request rule

The server applies the first rule that fits. A request that passes by rules 1 to 5 has no claims entry.

1. `GET` and `HEAD` requests to `/fhir/metadata` pass.
2. A request to a path under `/fhir/.well-known/` passes.
3. A request that fits an entry of `app.auth.public-endpoints` passes.
4. A paging request passes with no token. A paging request has the parameter `_getpages`. HAPI FHIR sends it to its paging handler.
5. A request that has the bypass header passes.
6. Each other request needs the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
7. The server validates the signature, the issuer, the `exp` claim, the `nbf` claim when present, and the audience when configured. The `exp` claim is necessary. The checks of `exp` and `nbf` allow a clock difference of 60 seconds. The token must use RS256, RS384, ES256, or ES384. When the token has a `typ` header, it must be `JWT` or `at+jwt`.
8. After a valid token, the server puts the verified claims in the user data of the request.

## Responses

- No token: status 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer`.
- A token that fails: status 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"`. The server log has the reason in one INFO line.
- The server cannot check the token: status 503 with no `WWW-Authenticate` header. This occurs when the read of the discovery document or of the key set fails, when the discovery document names a different issuer or has no `jwks_uri`, when `app.auth.jwks-uri` is not a valid URL, or when the key source refuses a new read of the key set. The key source allows two reads of the key set in 30 seconds. The server log has the reason in one WARN line.
- The body is an `OperationOutcome` with a fixed text. Its issue code is `security` for status 401 and `transient` for status 503.

## Claims in a custom interceptor

The check runs at the HAPI FHIR pointcut `SERVER_INCOMING_REQUEST_PRE_HANDLER_SELECTED`. A hook at a later pointcut reads the claims from `RequestDetails`. The key is `BearerTokenInterceptor.CLAIMS_KEY`, with the value `auth.claims`.

`requestDetails.getUserData().get(BearerTokenInterceptor.CLAIMS_KEY)` returns the verified claims as an unmodifiable `Map<String, Object>`. Cast the value to that type. For a request that passed with no token, the value is `null`.
